PURPOSE
=======
The purpose of this program is rather simple in nature.  There are three main
programs that provide three basic speaking functions for C-Net Amiga:

  o  SpeakerLogon.v2.1     - Announces when users log on
  o  SpeakerLogoff.v2.1    - Announces when users log off
  o  SpeakerChat.v2.1      - Announces when users page the SysOp.


SETUP AND CONFIGURATION
=======================
The programs are rather simple to setup for use with C-Net Amiga.

PLEASE NOTE:  SpeakerChat.v2.1 is written for C-Net Versions 2.18 and HIGHER
              It may >NOT< function properly on earlier versions of C-Net.

For all of the speaker programs to operate, you must have the Speak handler
mounted.  You will need to have an entry such as the following in your
mountlist in your DEVS: directory:

SPEAK:  Handler = L:Speak-Handler
        Stacksize = 4000
        Priority = 5
        GlobVec = -1
#

In addition to this mountlist entry, you will need the Speak-Handler itself
in the LIBS: directory.  Add to your startup-sequence file the command
MOUNT SPEAK: to have the Speak-Handler mounted and ready for use.

After that is done, you are ready to modify C-Net to run the speaker programs.
Get out your trusty text editor, load up BBSTEXT, and make these changes:

Line 99
Reads      :  @ Logoff complete\n1
Change To  :  \#0PFiles:SpeakLogoff.v2.1\@ Logoff complete\n1

Line 159
Reads      :  \n1Enter a reason for wanting to chat.\n1
Change To  :  \#0PFiles:SpeakerChat.v2.1\

Line 162
Reads      :  \n1Now paging the Sysop!\$0sounds SysData:chat.snd\\n1
Change To  :  \n1

Add to your SysText:sys.Welcome file the line:

   \#0PFiles:SpeakerLogon.v2.1\

Make sure you copy over all the programs into the PFiles: directory!
Reboot your board, and make sure all the programs work.


SPEAKER CONFIGURATION PROGRAM
=============================
With the release of Version 2.1 of the Speaker utilities is also the
SpeakerConfig.v2.1 utility.  This is a regular ARexx program for use by
SysOps to configure the way each of the Speaker programs sound.  To install
this program, copy it to your PFiles: directory and add it in as an ARexx
program - this program should only be accessable to the SysOp(s).  The
following explains the proper usage of this utility:

Each option on the opening menu does:

  1.  Configuring program - tells you which program you are currently
      setting the configuration for.  Selecting this item will allow you
      to set the configuration for any of the Speaker utilities.  ONCE YOU
      SELECT THIS OPTION, YOU WILL LOSE ANY CHANGES THAT YOU DID >NOT< SAVE!

  2.  Delete Configuration - deletes the configuration file for the Speaker
      program in menu item one.  This will effectively reset the configuration
      file (when there isn't one).  The Speaker utilities will work fine
      without a configuration file.

  3.  Save Current Configuration - saves the current configuration for the
      Speaker utility given in menu item one.  The configuration file will
      be placed in the PFiles: directory.

  4.  Pitch - this will display out to the right the current pitch for the
      given configuration.  If there isn't anything set, it will be displayed
      as "NONE SET."  Selecting this item will allow you to set the pitch.

  5.  Speed - this operates identically the same as the pitch option.

  6.  Voice - displays the current voice setting.  Selecting this option
      will allow you to set the Speaker voice to Male, Female, Robot, or
      Natural.

  7.  Test/Output Current Configuration - this will Speak the current
      configuration set up.  This will allow you to perfect the sound the
      way you like it from within the configuration program.

  8.  Sound - this will set the global flag (a file called CNet:Sound)
      that will mute ALL the Speaker utilites at once.  You can not mute
      the Speaker utilities individually.

  9.  Quit - Exits the Configuration program without saving.


USAGE NOTES
===========
There are SEVERAL usage notes of interest; they are as follows:

  o  Sometimes, you may want to run applications in the background to C-Net
     that will output sound.  Sometimes, if the SPEAK: device is activated
     or is interrupted by other programs that output sound, it can cause the
     Speak-Handler to freeze.  This >may< not be a problem for you; however,
     provisions have been made to turn OFF the Speaker programs without
     having to alter the programs or BBSTEXT.  By simply making a file in
     the CNET: directory called SOUND, this can be accomplished.  With a
     text editor, if you put the word OFF (case doesn't matter) into the
     file CNet:Sound, it will turn off the Speaker program output until
     you either kill the CNet:Sound file or place the word ON (or any other
     word besides off) into it.  Placing the following examples into the
     CNet:Sound file will do as described:

         ON       - Programs will speak
         OFF      - Programs will NOT speak
         off      - Programs will NOT speak
         Off      - Programs will NOT speak
         Off!     - Programs >WILL< speak

     You can use this to quiet the system while you are sleeping, using
     other sound applications, etc.  Deleting the CNET:Sound file is not
     an error - this assumes you want the sound to be on.  This can now be
     set with the SpeakerConfig program.

  o  You may notice some QUIRKYNESS of the SpeakerChat.v2.1 program.  Let me
     explain a few things about its operations.  When this program is run,
     it circumvents C-Net's SysOp Page Entry Reason; however, earlier
     versions of this program would require users to either give his/her
     reason twice or press return through C-Net's reason prompt.  This has
     been completely changed in Version 2.  The first twenty-nine characters
     or so of the SpeakerChat Reason will be placed into C-Net's own SysOp
     Page Reason Prompt; therefore, users >MAY< see part of his/her reason
     twice - that's just one of the hazards to modifying C-Net.  It's nothing
     you can't live with.  You will also notice that there are TWO reasons
     added to the Log Of Callers (LC) IF you have ARexx Log Entry turned ON
     in the BBSCONFIG.  All this is done so the SysOp can see the full
     chat reason line in the Log Of Callers plus see part of the chat reason
     in the upper right hand corner of the users port window after the page.

  o  Please note that you WILL need to have SysOp IN checkmarked on from the
     control panel for the SpeakerChat.v2.1 to work properly!

  o  There is also a ChatRanQuote.v2 program included to use in conjunction
     with the SpeakerChat.v2.1 program.  It provides a random quote to be
     displayed after each chat attempt as well as making the output from
     the SpeakerChat.v2.1 program look a little cleaner on the user side.
     Add this line to your BBSTEXT file to use the ChatRanQuote.v2 program:

     Line 162
     Reads      :  \n1Now paging the Sysop!\$0sound SysData:chat.snd\\n1
     Change To  :  \#0PFiles:ChatRanQuote.v2\\n1

     The program assumes that you have a directory called PFiles:Chat that
     contains text files of the type ChatMsg001, ChatMsg002, ...,
     ChatMsg010 (the program currently expects ten messages).  Each file
     can be of any length, and they can be created/edited with any text
     editor.  Examples for these files have been included in the archive,
     and you NEED to review these quotes before using them - some SysOps
     may want to change a few of them.

     NOTE:  If the program is unable to open or find a file, it will not
            report an error, but exit gracefully.

  o  All of the programs have appropriate checks for carrier where necessary.
     They are fully robust and >should< not cause any error; however, in the
     event of some unforeseen happening, the programs will add to the Log
     Of Callers it's program name, error number, error type, and line number
     that the error occurred.  For this to work properly, you will need to
     make sure your BBSCONFIG has ARexx turned on for the Log Of Calls (this
     is line number seven for C-Net Version 2.19; for the Log Of Calls to
     add ARexx, line number seven should be an ODD number (1, 3, 5, etc.)).

  o  If you use our AutoMod programs, included at the end of this
     documentation are some AutoMod blocks that you can cut and paste
     into your BBSTEXT.AutoMod file.

  o  You may not want maintenance users (i.e. local logons) to announce
     logons and logoffs verbally.  You can easily accommodate this by using
     the following MCI command:

          \t61\\e1\#0PFiles:SpeakerLog???.v2.1\\m1

     Note that maintenance users that LOGON remotely do not get maintenance
     access until after logging on and using the ID command; therefore,
     remote SysOp's will be announced.  You can bypass access groups if you
     would rather with a similar command of:

          \t523\\e1\#0PFiles:SpeakerLog???.v2.1\\m1

  o  A previous release of SpeakerChat (version 2.0) had a minor glitch
     that would make the C-Net appear to be hung if the user aborted the
     chat with a blank line.  That oversight has been cleared up with the
     release of SpeakerChat.v2.1.


